    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Since $AB$ is the diameter of the circle, the tangent at $B$ is perpendicular to $AB$. Let this tangent line intersect the tangent line at $E$ at a point $F$. From this point $F$, draw a radius of the circle, so the line $AF$ is a diameter of the circle and $F$ is the center of the circle. Since $F$ is the center of the circle, $AF$ is perpendicular to the tangent at $E$, so it is the perpendicular bisector of the tangent segment at $E$.\n\nSince $DE$ is perpendicular to the tangent at $E$, we can draw $DF$ perpendicular to $DE$, since this intersection point $F$ lies on the diameter $AF$ of the circle. Then $\\triangle DEF$ is similar to $\\triangle AEB$, since corresponding angles are congruent and $DE$ is the length of $AE$. $\\angle EDF$ is the exterior angle, so $\\angle AEX = \\angle EDF$, making $\\angle AEX$ external to the triangle $AEF$, so $\\angel AEX$, $\\angle DEF$ and $\\angle BAE$ are all exterior angles. Substituting the angle measures, we set up the equation:\n$\\angle DEF = x \\angle BAE = x \\cdot 43^\\circ.\n\nSince $\\triangle DEF$ is similar to $\\triangle AEB$, $\\frac{ER}{ER + RF} = \\frac{ED}{EB}$, and both $ED$ and $EB$ are equal to $RF$, so $ER = ER$, and we can simplify the equation to $\\frac{ER}{ER} = \\frac{53^\\circ}{97^\\circ}$.\nCalculating, we get $\\angle ECD = 22.5^\\circ$. The given problem only asks for the measure of $\\angel CED$, so $\\boxed{\\angle CED \\:=\\:22.5^\\circ}.$.",
